The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<code>
start = PAGE_ALIGN_DOWN(efi.unaccepted);
size = PAGE_ALIGN(sizeof(*unaccepted) + unaccepted->size);
memblock_reserve(start, size);
</code>
Reason:
The size is page-aligned but the start is aligned down. When `efi.unaccepted` is not page-aligned, the calculated end (`start + size`) may be less than the actual end (`efi.unaccepted + sizeof(*unaccepted) + unaccepted->size`). This causes the last part of the table to remain unreserved, leading to potential memory corruption or kernel panic.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<code>
start = PAGE_ALIGN_DOWN(efi.unaccepted);
end = PAGE_ALIGN(efi.unaccepted + sizeof(*unaccepted) + unaccepted->size);
memblock_reserve(start, end - start);
</code>
Reason:
By first computing the actual end address and then aligning it up, the reserved range spans from the page-aligned start to the page-aligned end, ensuring the entire table (including any unaligned offset) is fully covered.

